@page "/live-state"

<CatHead Title="Live states">
    What a view shows while something is still happening: the state of the connection behind it,
    the lines a job is writing, how long ago something changed or how long is left, and work the
    reader can watch and cancel. A view with nothing to
    show yet is an <a href="/empty-state">empty state</a>; a wait with a known shape is a
    <a href="/progress">skeleton</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.LiveState.Connection" Title="Connection state" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.health-badge</code> with a <code>.health-dot</code>, in six states: the active
        <code>--live</code>, <code>--streaming</code> and <code>--connecting</code> pulse, and the
        settled <code>--idle</code>, <code>--degraded</code> and <code>--down</code> do not.
        <strong>The word is not optional</strong> &mdash; a colour alone fails for anybody who
        cannot see it. <code>.stale</code> is how old the number beside it is, with
        <code>--warn</code> when it is old enough to distrust. The circuit's own state is the
        <a href="/status-bar">status bar</a>'s job, not a badge's.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.LiveState.OutputPane" Title="Output pane" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output</code> is lines of machine output &mdash; a build log, a command's stdout, an
        event stream, an audit trail &mdash; as a real list, monospace with tabular figures, with
        <code>.output-head</code> outside the pane so it does not scroll away with the lines.
        <strong>Add <code>data-follow</code> and it sticks to the newest line</strong>, releasing
        when the reader scrolls up and re-attaching when they scroll back down;
        <code>sednaUi.output.follow(pane)</code> re-attaches it from a &ldquo;jump to
        latest&rdquo; button. The pane scrolls past 320px; <code>--output-max-height</code> on it
        sets another height, or <code>none</code>. An application log in columns, with levels, rows that expand and
        actions per line, is on <a href="/logs">Logs</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.LiveState.RelativeList" Title="Relative time in a list" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>&lt;time datetime data-relative&gt;</code> keeps &ldquo;how long ago&rdquo; current
        from one timer for the whole page, which sleeps until the next whole unit and stops while
        the tab is hidden. <strong>The words are the app's</strong>: a format per unit &mdash;
        <code>data-seconds</code>, <code>data-minutes</code>, <code>data-hours</code>,
        <code>data-days</code>, <code>data-weeks</code>, <code>data-months</code>,
        <code>data-years</code> &mdash; with <code>{n}</code> where the number goes, and one per
        plural category of the page's language where it needs them: <code>data-days-one</code>,
        and <code>-two</code>, <code>-few</code>, <code>-many</code> or <code>-zero</code>. Sedna.UI.js
        picks the unit and writes the number in that language. A unit with no format is counted in
        the next smaller one; an element with no formats is left as written. Write the absolute
        time inside, for a reader without the script, and in <code>data-tip</code> or
        <code>title</code>; keep the element to its text, and put an icon beside it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.LiveState.RelativeTable" Title="Relative time in a table" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same element in a cell, with formats short enough for a column &mdash;
        <code>{n} min</code>, <code>{n} h</code>. The counts round down: an order updated
        ninety seconds ago is one minute ago. A time a little ahead of the reader's clock reads
        as the smallest unit at zero until it passes.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.SessionCountdown" Title="Countdown" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-countdown</code> instead of <code>data-relative</code> counts down to the
        <code>datetime</code>, in the same formats. It never reads zero before it is zero &mdash;
        under its smallest unit it shows 1 &mdash; and at zero it dispatches a bubbling
        <code>sedna-countdown-end</code> with <code>detail.until</code>, once per element and
        <code>datetime</code>: also for a countdown already over when it is found, and when the
        reader comes back to a tab hidden while it ended. In Blazor it is
        <code>@@onsedna-countdown-end</code> with a <code>SednaCountdownEventArgs</code>. Render
        a later <code>datetime</code> and it counts again. The deadline is the server's: the
        countdown tells the reader, it does not end the session.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.LiveState.ActivityRow" Title="Work in progress" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Background work the reader can see and cancel. There is no activity-row class: it is a
        <a href="/list"><code>.list-row</code></a> with a
        <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a> bar in its subtext &mdash; determinate for
        work with a known size, indeterminate for work accepted but not started &mdash; and the
        controls in <code>.list-meta</code>. Mark the row <code>aria-busy="true"</code> while it
        runs.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.LiveState.Degraded" Title="Degraded page" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        When a service behind a page is down, the page keeps working around it. <strong>Replace each
        failed part where it stands</strong>: a figure that could not be read is
        <code>.stat--unavailable</code> with a <code>.stale</code> saying how old the last good one is, a
        panel that could not load is an <code>.empty-state--failed</code> in its own card with a retry,
        and every part that did load shows as usual. Say what is down once &mdash; a
        <a href="/banner"><code>.page-banner</code></a> under the topbar, and at most a few outage
        messages on a page &mdash; and what still works. <strong>Never hide the navigation, and never
        disable a control because a service is down</strong>: the reader can still dispatch an order,
        and an action that fails says so when it is taken.
    </p>
</CatExample>
